---
// H1–H2: one stubborn person in a room that runs a consensus-seeking rule; the room becomes a toy network (the people
// lose their bodies and their heads scatter into it), where a quiet node changes nothing and an important one flips
// everyone (each run waits for its own start); then every node stubborn in turn, for its dynamical importance (the
// thesis says clamped; the slides never do). H3: what it could be for: a health campaign with three ambassadors and an
// unknown rule of persuasion, the consensus-seeking rule as a stand-in, and our result. The limitations are for the
// questions, not the slides.
// Data: src/data/defence/clamp.json.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import Seats from './Seats.astro';
import SimButton from './SimButton.astro';
import clamp from '../../data/defence/clamp.json';
import { knnSpearman } from '../../scripts/defence/importance';
const knnRho = knnSpearman(clamp);
---
<Slide id="ch-impact" label="The stubborn node" builds={6} class="h1" ch={10}>
  <div class="row grow h1-row">
    <div class="stack h1-view">
      <div class="h1-seats" data-b-off="1"><Seats label="The room with every hand up except one" /></div>
      <div class="h1-box" data-b="1">
        <canvas class="net" aria-label="A network of 100 people running a consensus-seeking rule, one of them stubborn"></canvas>
        <SimButton class="h1-btn" />
      </div>
      <canvas class="h1-morph" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="col h1-side">
      <div class="h1-head">
        <h2>The stubborn node</h2>
        <p class="h1-sub">Which individuals steer the group?</p>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">Everyone follows a consensus-seeking rule, and the room ends with every hand up.</p>
      <p class="lede">Now one person refuses: <span class="danger">hand down, no matter what</span>. Does the room follow?</p>
      <p class="h1-state" data-state></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">The second application uses the consensus-seeking rules that were such bad detectives. Imagine everyone in the room follows one: after a few timesteps every hand is up. Now one person refuses and keeps their hand down, whatever happens. Does the room follow them?</p>
  <p slot="say">Now the room becomes a network: the people lose their bodies, and their heads scatter into a network of 100 people, half of them with a hand up. With nobody stubborn, it ends with every hand up. When a quiet person at the edge is stubborn (ringed), nobody else follows. When an important person is stubborn, the whole network flips to every hand down. Holding one node fixed like this is called clamping, or pinning: it is an intervention, not just an observation. (An illustration: a consensus-seeking rule on a scale-free network of 100 people, about eight contacts each, from the same start every time.)</p>
</Slide>

<Slide label="Dynamical importance score" builds={2} class="h2" ch={10}>
  <h2>Dynamical importance score</h2>
  <p class="h2-sub">Analysing the impact of individuals</p>
  <div class="row grow h2-row">
    <canvas class="net" aria-label="The network with every node coloured by how much of the network follows it when it is stubborn"></canvas>
    <div class="col h2-side">
      <p class="h2-def"><b>Importance</b> (darker): how much of the network follows a stubborn node, on average.</p>
      <div class="stack h2-plots">
        <canvas class="hist" data-b-off="1" aria-label="The share of nodes with each importance"></canvas>
        <div class="h2-pair" data-b="1">
          <canvas class="scatter" aria-label="Importance against the degree"></canvas>
          <canvas class="scatter" data-b="2" aria-label="Importance against the average degree of the neighbours"></canvas>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">Now make every node stubborn in turn, one at a time, and record how much of the network ends up following it: that is each node’s dynamical importance score (in the thesis, holding a node fixed is called clamping). On this network most nodes change almost nothing, and a few flip the whole network most of the time. (Chapter 10, work in progress; an illustration on the network of the previous slide, over {clamp.summary.starts} starts that reach agreement, with each node stubborn in each.)</p>
  <p slot="say">Against the degree, the number of links, importance clearly goes up, but not one-to-one (a rank correlation of {clamp.summary.spearmanEtaDegree.toFixed(2)} on this network). Against the average degree of a node’s neighbours there is no relation at all ({knnRho.toFixed(2)}). In the thesis that measure is consistently the weakest, and on some scale-free networks even negative: a node whose neighbours are hubs matters less, because each hub hears from many others. Across families of networks the score goes with the usual measures of centrality but reduces to none of them.</p>
</Slide>

<Slide label="Who should we convince first?" builds={3} class="h3" ch={10}>
  <h2>Who should we convince first?</h2>
  <p class="h3-sub">A health campaign with a small budget</p>
  <div class="row grow h3-row">
    <canvas class="net" aria-label="A village as a network; then every villager coloured by their importance under a stand-in rule; then the three most important ringed"></canvas>
    <div class="col h3-side">
      <p class="h3-line">Budget: <b>three ambassadors</b></p>
      <p class="h3-line">How villagers persuade each other: <b>unknown</b></p>
      <p class="h3-line" data-b="1">Stand-in: a <b>consensus-seeking rule</b></p>
      <p class="result" data-b="3"><b>Our result</b> Rank who steers the group without knowing the real rule, using a stand-in rule.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">Why would we want to know who matters? Think of a health campaign with a small budget. In 32 villages in Honduras, researchers started a water-purification and a vitamin programme with a few villagers each, to see how far it would spread, and compared ways of picking those few (Kim and colleagues, The Lancet, 2015). So which three villagers should be our ambassadors? We do not know how villagers really persuade each other: the real rule is unknown.</p>
  <p slot="say">So we use a stand-in: the consensus-seeking rule, not to model the villagers, but to measure them. Make each villager stubborn in turn and see how far the village follows: the darker, the further. (An illustration: the network of the previous slides, standing in for a village.)</p>
  <p slot="say">The three darkest become our ambassadors: an ambassador is a stubborn node, who keeps promoting the programme, no matter what. On this small network they are also its three biggest hubs, and neighbours of each other: taking the top three one by one ignores that they may reach the same people, and choosing them together is a next step.</p>
  <p slot="say">Our result: a ranking of who steers the group that needs no knowledge of the real rule and no measure of centrality, only a stand-in rule and a simulator. Whether the stand-in picks the same people as the real rule would is the most important next step of Chapter 10: comparing it with, say, an epidemic model on the same networks. This part of the thesis is unpublished and still in progress.</p>
</Slide>

<style>
  .h1-row { gap: calc(60 * var(--u)); align-items: center; }
  .h1-view { width: calc(900 * var(--u)); height: calc(900 * var(--u)); flex: none; place-items: center; }
  .h1-seats :global(.seats) { height: calc(880 * var(--u)); width: auto; }
  .h1-box { position: relative; }
  .h1-box canvas { display: block; width: calc(840 * var(--u)); height: calc(840 * var(--u)); }
  .h1-box :global(.h1-btn) { right: calc(10 * var(--u)); bottom: calc(10 * var(--u)); }
  .h1-morph { width: calc(900 * var(--u)); height: calc(900 * var(--u)); pointer-events: none; visibility: hidden; }
  /* while the people turn into the network, the network itself waits under the picture that moves */
  :global(.h1.morphing) .h1-morph { visibility: visible; }
  :global(.h1.morphing) .h1-box canvas { visibility: hidden; }
  .h1-side { gap: calc(34 * var(--u)); }
  .h1-head { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: calc(14 * var(--u)); margin-bottom: calc(16 * var(--u)); }
  .h1-sub { font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); }
  .h1-state { font-size: calc(34 * var(--u)); min-height: 3em; }

  .h2-sub { font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(-12 * var(--u)); }
  .h2-row { gap: calc(50 * var(--u)); align-items: flex-start; }
  .h2-row > canvas.net { width: calc(660 * var(--u)); height: calc(660 * var(--u)); flex: none; }
  .h2-side { gap: calc(30 * var(--u)); flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
  .h2-def { font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); line-height: 1.25; }
  .h2-plots { width: 100%; }
  .hist { width: 100%; height: calc(470 * var(--u)); }
  .h2-pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: calc(16 * var(--u)); }
  .scatter { width: 100%; height: calc(470 * var(--u)); }

  .h3-sub { font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(-12 * var(--u)); }
  .h3-row { gap: calc(60 * var(--u)); align-items: center; }
  .h3-row > canvas.net { width: calc(660 * var(--u)); height: calc(660 * var(--u)); flex: none; }
  .h3-side { gap: calc(36 * var(--u)); flex: 1; }
  .h3-line { font-size: calc(46 * var(--u)); line-height: 1.25; }
  :global(.h3) .result { margin-top: calc(20 * var(--u)); }
</style>

<script>
  import { slide, sim, type How } from '../../scripts/defence/deck';
  import { stage, onSize, ticker } from '../../scripts/defence/stage';
  import { axes, dot, px, py, type Plot } from '../../scripts/defence/plot';
  import { neighbourDegree } from '../../scripts/defence/importance';
  import { type Rule, density } from '../../scripts/llna';
  import { stepClamped } from '../../scripts/consensus';
  import { build } from '../../scripts/net';
  import { parse, mix, css, type RGB } from '../../scripts/palette';
  import clamp from '../../data/defence/clamp.json';

  const RULE: Rule = { r: 9, B: 464, S: 488 };
  const n = clamp.deg.length, net = build('ba', n, clamp.edges as [number, number][]);
  const X = (i: number, b: { x: number; w: number }) => b.x + clamp.xy[2 * i] * b.w, Y = (i: number, b: { y: number; h: number }) => b.y + clamp.xy[2 * i + 1] * b.h;
  const radius = (i: number) => 7 + 1.6 * Math.sqrt(clamp.deg[i]);

  /** The network: faint links, then the people, filled when their hand is up; a ringed person last, over the rest. */
  function drawNet(st: NonNullable<ReturnType<typeof stage>>, state: (i: number) => string, ring: (i: number) => string | null) {
    const { ctx, t } = st, b = { x: 30, y: 30, w: st.w - 60, h: st.h - 60 };
    ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.lineWidth = 1.5; ctx.beginPath();
    for (const [i, j] of clamp.edges) { ctx.moveTo(X(i, b), Y(i, b)); ctx.lineTo(X(j, b), Y(j, b)); }
    ctx.stroke();
    const circle = (i: number, r: number, width: number, color: string) => { ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(X(i, b), Y(i, b), r, 0, 2 * Math.PI); ctx.lineWidth = width; ctx.strokeStyle = color; ctx.stroke(); };
    const person = (i: number) => { ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(X(i, b), Y(i, b), radius(i), 0, 2 * Math.PI); ctx.fillStyle = state(i); ctx.fill(); circle(i, radius(i), 2, t.muted); };
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!ring(i)) person(i);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { const r = ring(i); if (r) { person(i); circle(i, radius(i) + 10, 13, t.panel); circle(i, radius(i) + 10, 6, r); } }
  }

  // ── H1: the room, then the network: nobody stubborn (shown at build 1, run at 2), a quiet one (3, 4), an important
  // one (5, 6) ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.h1')) {
    const c = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.net')!, out = s.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-state]')!;
    const svg = s.querySelector<SVGSVGElement>('svg.seats')!, seats = Array.from(svg.querySelectorAll<SVGUseElement>('use.seat'));
    for (const u of seats) { u.setAttribute('href', '#hand-up'); u.classList.replace('down', 'up'); }
    seats[3 * 8 + 5].setAttribute('href', '#hand-down'); seats[3 * 8 + 5].classList.replace('up', 'stubborn');
    const start = Uint8Array.from(clamp.demo.bits, (ch) => +ch), { hi, lo } = clamp.demo;
    let who = -1, a = start.slice(), b = new Uint8Array(n), tick = 0, cl = new Int8Array(n).fill(-1), done = false, at = 0;
    function reset(k: number) {
      who = k; cl.fill(-1); a = start.slice(); tick = 0; done = false;
      if (k >= 0) { cl[k] = 0; a[k] = 0; }
    }
    function paint() {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      const { t } = st;
      drawNet(st, (i) => (a[i] ? t.ink : t.panel), (i) => (i === who ? t.danger : null));
      const up = Math.round(density(a) * n);
      if (at === 0) { out.textContent = ''; return; }
      out.innerHTML = `timestep ${tick} · ${up} of ${n} hands up` + (done ? (up <= 0 ? '<br><b class="danger">the whole room followed</b>' : who >= 0 ? '<br>nobody else followed' : '<br>consensus: every hand up') : '');
    }
    const advance = () => {
      stepClamped(a, net, RULE, cl, b); [a, b] = [b, a]; tick++;
      const r = density(a);
      if (tick >= 40 || r === 0 || r === 1 || (who >= 0 && r >= 1 - 1.5 / n && tick > 12)) { done = true; return true; }
      return false;
    };
    const run = ticker(s, 3, advance, paint);
    // ── the room becomes the network (build 0 → 1): the bodies fade while the heads stay, then each head flies to a node
    // of its own, nearest first, as the links come in; the network's other twenty nodes grow where they are. On a
    // canvas over both pictures, from where the seats and the network are laid out on screen. ──
    const mc = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.h1-morph')!, MORPH_MS = 1900;
    const BODY = new Path2D('M8 128 C8 96 20 74 42 74 C64 74 76 96 76 128 Z'), ARM = new Path2D('M65 93 C80 85 84.5 70 84.5 46');
    let morph = 1, morphRaf = 0;
    type Head = { x: number; y: number; w: number; up: boolean; fill: RGB; stroke: RGB; to: number };
    function morphPaint() {
      const st = stage(mc); if (!st) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, m = mc.getBoundingClientRect(), sv = svg.getBoundingClientRect(), nb = c.getBoundingClientRect(), k = st.s;
      // the seats (viewBox 880 wide; a seat's pictogram is 84 × 109 at column × 110 + 13, 96 + row × 128 + 8) and the
      // network's nodes (Stubborn's drawNet: 30 in from its canvas's edges), in the morph canvas's design pixels
      const q = sv.width / 880 / k, sx = (sv.left - m.left) / k, sy = (sv.top - m.top) / k, nx = (nb.left - m.left) / k, ny = (nb.top - m.top) / k;
      const box = { x: 30, y: 30, w: nb.width / k - 60, h: nb.height / k - 60 };
      const heads: Head[] = seats.map((u, i) => {
        const cs = getComputedStyle(u), col = i % 8, row = Math.floor(i / 8);
        return { x: sx + (col * 110 + 13) * q, y: sy + (96 + row * 128 + 8) * q, w: 84 * q, up: u.getAttribute('href') === '#hand-up', fill: parse(cs.fill), stroke: parse(cs.stroke), to: -1 };
      });
      const node = (j: number) => ({ x: nx + X(j, box), y: ny + Y(j, box) });
      // nearest first: every head its own node
      const pairs: [number, number, number][] = [], taken = new Uint8Array(n);
      heads.forEach((h, i) => { const hx = h.x + 0.42 * h.w, hy = h.y + 0.4 * h.w; for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) { const p = node(j); pairs.push([(p.x - hx) ** 2 + (p.y - hy) ** 2, i, j]); } });
      pairs.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
      for (const [, i, j] of pairs) if (heads[i].to < 0 && !taken[j]) { heads[i].to = j; taken[j] = 1; }
      const ink = parse(t.ink), panel = parse(t.panel), muted = parse(t.muted);
      const frame = () => {
        const fade = Math.min(1, morph / 0.22), g = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (morph - 0.22) / 0.78)), e = g < 0.5 ? 2 * g * g : 1 - (-2 * g + 2) ** 2 / 2;
        // the heads keep their colours in flight and take the start's as they land
        const land = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (g - 0.6) / 0.4)), hue = land * land * (3 - 2 * land);
        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, st.w, st.h);
        // where every node is now: a head on its way, or one of the twenty that grow in place
        const px = new Float32Array(n), py = new Float32Array(n);
        for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) { const p = node(j); px[j] = p.x; py[j] = p.y; }
        for (const h of heads) { const p = node(h.to), hx = h.x + 0.42 * h.w, hy = h.y + 0.4 * h.w; px[h.to] = hx + (p.x - hx) * e; py[h.to] = hy + (p.y - hy) * e; }
        if (e > 0) {
          ctx.globalAlpha = e; ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.lineWidth = 1.5; ctx.beginPath();
          for (const [i, j] of clamp.edges) { ctx.moveTo(px[i], py[i]); ctx.lineTo(px[j], py[j]); }
          ctx.stroke(); ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
        }
        // the screen and the bodies, fading (a person is 100 × 130 in its pictogram's units)
        if (fade < 1) {
          ctx.globalAlpha = 1 - fade; ctx.fillStyle = t.line; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.roundRect(sx + 60 * q, sy + 12 * q, 760 * q, 24 * q, 4 * q); ctx.fill();
          ctx.fillStyle = t.muted; ctx.font = `${34 * q}px ${t.mono}`; ctx.textAlign = 'center'; ctx.textBaseline = 'alphabetic'; ctx.fillText('screen', sx + 440 * q, sy + 78 * q); ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
        }
        if (fade < 1) for (const h of heads) {
          ctx.save(); ctx.translate(h.x, h.y); ctx.scale(h.w / 100, h.w / 100); ctx.globalAlpha = 1 - fade;
          ctx.fillStyle = css(h.fill); ctx.strokeStyle = css(h.stroke);
          ctx.fill(BODY);
          if (h.up) { ctx.lineWidth = 12.5; ctx.lineCap = 'round'; ctx.stroke(ARM); ctx.beginPath(); ctx.ellipse(84.5, 31, 10, 14, 0, 0, 2 * Math.PI); ctx.fill(); }
          else { ctx.lineWidth = 3; ctx.stroke(BODY); }
          ctx.restore();
        }
        // the twenty nodes nobody sat on, growing
        for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) if (!taken[j] && e > 0) {
          ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(px[j], py[j], radius(j) * e, 0, 2 * Math.PI); ctx.fillStyle = start[j] ? t.ink : t.panel; ctx.fill();
          ctx.lineWidth = 2; ctx.strokeStyle = t.muted; ctx.stroke();
        }
        // the heads, turning into nodes of the start: half of them on
        for (const h of heads) {
          const j = h.to, r0 = 0.17 * h.w;
          ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(px[j], py[j], r0 + (radius(j) - r0) * e, 0, 2 * Math.PI);
          ctx.fillStyle = css(mix(h.fill, start[j] ? ink : panel, hue)); ctx.fill();
          ctx.lineWidth = (h.up ? 0 : 3 * h.w / 100) * (1 - hue) + 2 * hue; if (ctx.lineWidth > 0.05) { ctx.strokeStyle = css(mix(h.stroke, muted, hue)); ctx.stroke(); }
        }
      };
      return frame;
    }
    function becomeNetwork() {
      const t0 = performance.now();
      s.classList.add('morphing'); morph = 0;
      const frame = morphPaint();
      if (!frame) { stopMorph(); return; }
      const tick = (now: number) => {
        morph = Math.min(1, (now - t0) / MORPH_MS); frame();
        if (morph < 1) morphRaf = requestAnimationFrame(tick); else stopMorph();
      };
      frame(); morphRaf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }
    function stopMorph() { cancelAnimationFrame(morphRaf); morphRaf = 0; morph = 1; s.classList.remove('morphing'); }
    const nodeAt = (k: number) => (k >= 5 ? hi : k >= 3 ? lo : -1);
    sim(s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!, [2, 4, 6], { reset() { run.play(false); reset(nodeAt(at)); paint(); }, play() { run.play(true); } });
    slide(s, {
      // going back to a run shows how it ended; the button's waiting builds reset it
      set(k: number, how: How) {
        const was = at; at = k;
        stopMorph();
        if (k === 1 && was === 0 && how === 'next') becomeNetwork();
        if (nodeAt(k) !== who || how !== 'next') { run.play(false); reset(nodeAt(k)); if (how === 'prev' && k % 2 === 0 && k > 0) while (!advance()); }
        paint();
      },
      enter() { reset(-1); },
      leave() { run.play(false); stopMorph(); },
    });
    onSize(c, paint);
  }

  // ── H2: everyone's importance on the network; its spread, as a share of the nodes; then against two measures of the
  // same network's structure: the degree, which goes with it, and the average degree of a node's neighbours, which
  // does not (./importance) ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.h2')) {
    const nc = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.net')!, hc = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.hist')!;
    const [dc, kc] = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.scatter'));
    const eta = clamp.eta, top = Math.max(...eta), knn = neighbourDegree(clamp);
    onSize(nc, () => {
      const st = stage(nc); if (!st) return;
      const lo = parse(st.t.panel), hiC = parse(st.t.accent);
      drawNet(st, (i) => css(mix(lo, hiC, eta[i] / top)), () => null);
    });
    onSize(hc, () => {
      const st = stage(hc); if (!st) return;
      const bins = 16, pct = new Array(bins).fill(0);
      for (const v of eta) pct[Math.min(bins - 1, Math.floor((v / 0.8) * bins))] += 100 / n;
      const most = Math.ceil(Math.max(...pct) / 10) * 10;
      const p: Plot = { x: 150, y: 20, w: st.w - 180, h: st.h - 130, x0: 0, x1: 0.8, y0: 0, y1: most * 1.05 };
      axes(st, p, { xticks: [[0, 'none'], [0.8, 'high']], yticks: [[0, '0'], [most, String(most)]], xname: 'importance', yname: '% nodes' });
      pct.forEach((v, b) => { st.ctx.fillStyle = st.t.accent; st.ctx.fillRect(px(p, (b * 0.8) / bins) + 2, py(p, v), (p.w / bins) - 4, py(p, 0) - py(p, v)); });
    });
    // the right-hand plot shares the left one's importance axis, so it leaves out its ticks and name
    const scatter = (c: HTMLCanvasElement, xs: ArrayLike<number>, x0: number, x1: number, xticks: [number, string][], xname: string, left: boolean) => onSize(c, () => {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      const p: Plot = { x: left ? 150 : 40, y: 20, w: st.w - (left ? 180 : 70), h: st.h - 130, x0, x1, y0: 0, y1: 0.85 };
      axes(st, p, { xticks, yticks: left ? [[0, 'none'], [0.8, 'high']] : [], xname, yname: left ? 'importance' : '' });
      for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dot(st, px(p, xs[i]), py(p, eta[i]), 7, st.t.accent);
    });
    scatter(dc, clamp.deg, 0, Math.max(...clamp.deg) + 2, [[4, '4'], [10, '10'], [20, '20'], [30, '30']], 'degree', true);
    scatter(kc, knn, 6, 22, [[8, '8'], [12, '12'], [16, '16'], [20, '20']], 'average neighbour degree', false);
  }

  // ── H3: the village, plain (build 0); every villager in the colour of their importance under the stand-in rule, the
  // scores of H2 (build 1); the three most important ringed in red, as stubborn nodes are (build 2); our result (3) ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.h3')) {
    const c = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.net')!, eta = clamp.eta, top = Math.max(...eta);
    const three = new Set(eta.map((_, i) => i).sort((a, b) => eta[b] - eta[a]).slice(0, 3));
    let at = 0;
    const paint = () => {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      const lo = parse(st.t.panel), hiC = parse(st.t.accent);
      drawNet(st, (i) => (at >= 1 ? css(mix(lo, hiC, eta[i] / top)) : st.t.panel), (i) => (at >= 2 && three.has(i) ? st.t.danger : null));
    };
    slide(s, { set(b: number) { at = b; paint(); } });
    onSize(c, paint);
  }
</script>
